"""In-depth material for the Digital Logic lessons."""
from learn import deepen

deepen("dl-boolean-minimization",
       proofs=[
           ("Absorption, consensus and redundancy laws from the basic axioms",
            """**Absorption:** A + AB = A(1 + B) = A·1 = A, using the distributive law and B + 1 = 1. Dually A(A + B) = A. **Redundancy:** A + A′B = (A + A′)(A + B) = 1·(A + B) = A + B, using the distributive law of + over ·. **Consensus:** AB + A′C + BC = AB + A′C + BC(A + A′) = AB + A′C + ABC + A′BC = AB(1 + C) + A′C(1 + B) = AB + A′C: the term BC (the consensus of AB and A′C) is redundant. These three identities are the algebraic reasons behind K-map groupings and the removal of redundant prime implicants; they also give the hazard-free covers (adding the consensus term removes a static-1 hazard)."""),
           ("De Morgan's laws and the universality of NAND",
            """(A·B)′ = A′ + B′ because the two sides agree on all four input combinations (the only way A·B is 0 is that A or B is 0, i.e. A′ or B′ is 1). Dually (A + B)′ = A′B′. NAND is universal: A NAND A = A′ (a NOT); (A NAND B) NAND (A NAND B) = AB (an AND); and A + B = (A′·B′)′ = A′ NAND B′, an OR built from NOTs and one NAND. Hence any Boolean function can be built from NAND gates alone; by symmetry the same holds for NOR. {AND, OR} alone is not complete: with only monotone gates one can never produce a function such as NOT."""),
           ("A group of 2ᵏ adjacent cells eliminates k variables",
            """Two minterms that differ in exactly one variable, mᵢ = P·X and mⱼ = P·X′, merge: P·X + P·X′ = P(X + X′) = P, dropping the variable X. K-map adjacency (Gray code order, including wrap-around) guarantees that physically adjacent cells differ in one variable. A group of four cells is two pairs of pairs: merging each pair removes one variable, then the two resulting terms differ in one further variable and merge again, removing two variables in all. By induction a group of 2ᵏ cells arranged as a k-dimensional subcube removes k variables, leaving a product term with n − k literals. This is also the Quine–McCluskey step: combine terms that differ in one bit and replace the bit by a dash."""),
       ],
       notes=[
           ("Prime implicants, essential PIs and minimal covers",
            """An implicant is a product term that implies the function; a prime implicant cannot be shortened further. An essential prime implicant covers a minterm that no other prime implicant covers and must appear in every minimal cover. The minimal SOP is found by choosing all essential primes, then a smallest set of remaining primes covering the rest (Petrick's method makes this systematic). Don't-care terms may be included to enlarge groups but need not be covered. A function can have several different minimal covers of equal cost; the number of prime and essential prime implicants is unique. For Σm(0,1,2,5,6,7,8,9,10,14) there are 6 prime implicants and a minimal cover of 3 terms."""),
           ("Canonical forms and counting",
            """The minterm expansion lists all rows with output 1, the maxterm expansion all rows with output 0; f′ has the complementary minterms. There are 2^(2ⁿ) functions of n variables; a self-dual function has f(x′) = f(x)′ and there are 2^(2ⁿ⁻¹) of them (16 for n = 3). The XOR of n variables has 2ⁿ⁻¹ minterms and no simplification (every minterm is a prime implicant). Monotone (positive) functions need no negations; threshold functions are realised by one perceptron. Gate counts: a 2-input XOR needs four NAND gates; a 2:1 multiplexer needs 3 NAND gates plus an inverter."""),
       ])

deepen("dl-combinational-circuits",
       proofs=[
           ("The full adder equations",
            """Adding bits A, B and carry-in C gives a sum bit S and a carry-out Cₒ with A + B + C = 2Cₒ + S. The sum is 1 exactly when an odd number of the three inputs are 1, i.e. S = A ⊕ B ⊕ C. The carry-out is 1 exactly when at least two inputs are 1: Cₒ = AB + AC + BC = AB + C(A ⊕ B) (the second form is obtained by splitting the cases A = B and A ≠ B: if A = B = 1 the carry is 1; if A ≠ B the carry is C). The second form shows that a full adder is two half adders (A ⊕ B with its AND, then (A ⊕ B) ⊕ C with (A ⊕ B)·C) and an OR gate combining the two carries: 2 XOR + 2 AND + 1 OR = 5 gates."""),
           ("Ripple-carry delay versus carry-lookahead",
            """In an n-bit ripple-carry adder the carry out of stage i depends on the carry out of stage i − 1: Cᵢ₊₁ = Gᵢ + PᵢCᵢ, so the worst-case delay grows linearly: n stages × (carry delay per stage), typically 2 gate delays per stage, 2n in total. Carry lookahead defines generate Gᵢ = AᵢBᵢ and propagate Pᵢ = Aᵢ ⊕ Bᵢ and expands the recurrence: C₁ = G₀ + P₀C₀; C₂ = G₁ + P₁G₀ + P₁P₀C₀; C₃ = G₂ + P₂G₁ + P₂P₁G₀ + P₂P₁P₀C₀, … Every carry is then a two-level AND–OR expression of the inputs, available after a constant number of gate delays independent of n (but the gate fan-in and count grow, so large adders use groups of 4 bits with a second-level lookahead)."""),
           ("Implementing any function with a multiplexer",
            """A 2ⁿ:1 multiplexer outputs Y = Σ mᵢ·Iᵢ over the select combinations, so connecting the data inputs to constant 0 or 1 according to the truth table implements any n-variable function. To save inputs use n − 1 selects and feed the last variable C into the data inputs: for each combination of the select variables the function is, as a function of C alone, one of 0, 1, C or C′. For f(A, B, C) = Σm(1, 2, 4, 7) with selects A, B: AB = 00 gives minterms 0 and 1, f = C; AB = 01 minterms 2, 3 give C′; AB = 10 minterms 4, 5 give C′; AB = 11 minterms 6, 7 give C. The data inputs are C, C′, C′, C."""),
       ],
       notes=[
           ("Decoders, encoders and cascading",
            """An n:2ⁿ decoder outputs exactly one active line (a minterm generator); an OR gate on the right lines gives any function. With an enable input decoders cascade: a 5:32 decoder is built from four 3:8 decoders and one 2:4 decoder (the 2:4 selects which 3:8 is enabled). A priority encoder outputs the index of the highest-priority active input and a valid flag. A demultiplexer is a decoder whose enable carries the data. Multiplexer trees: a 2ⁿ:1 multiplexer can be built from 2ⁿ − 1 two-to-one multiplexers. Comparators use XNOR for bit equality and a cascade from the MSB for greater/less."""),
           ("ROM, PLA and PAL",
            """A ROM with n address lines and m outputs is a fixed AND plane (a full decoder of 2ⁿ minterms) feeding a programmable OR plane: it stores 2ⁿ × m bits and implements m functions of n variables. A PLA has programmable AND and OR planes, implementing only the product terms needed; a PAL has a programmable AND plane and a fixed OR plane. Sequential PLD structures add flip-flops. The number of fuse points of a PLA with n inputs, p product terms and m outputs is 2np + pm."""),
       ])

deepen("dl-number-systems",
       proofs=[
           ("Two's complement: negation and range",
            """In n bits the unsigned patterns 0 … 2ⁿ − 1 are interpreted so that a pattern x with top bit 1 represents x − 2ⁿ. Then −y is represented by 2ⁿ − y: flipping every bit of y gives (2ⁿ − 1) − y, and adding 1 gives 2ⁿ − y. Ordinary binary addition modulo 2ⁿ is therefore correct for signed numbers: (a mod 2ⁿ) + (b mod 2ⁿ) ≡ a + b. The representable range is −2ⁿ⁻¹ … 2ⁿ⁻¹ − 1; the most negative value −2ⁿ⁻¹ has no positive counterpart, so negating it overflows. One's complement and sign-magnitude have two zeros and the smaller range −(2ⁿ⁻¹ − 1) … 2ⁿ⁻¹ − 1."""),
           ("Signed overflow: carry into the sign bit differs from carry out",
            """Adding two numbers of the same sign can overflow; numbers of different signs cannot (the magnitude of the result is smaller than that of the larger operand). If both operands are non-negative, the sign bits are 0 and 0, so the sign bit of the result equals the carry into that position; overflow occurs exactly when that carry is 1 (the result looks negative) while the carry out of the sign position is 0, so carries into and out of the sign position differ. If both are negative (sign bits 1, 1) the carry out of the sign position is always 1, and overflow occurs when the carry into it is 0 (the result looks non-negative); again the carries differ. If the signs differ, the carry into the sign position equals the carry out, and there is no overflow. Hence overflow = C_in(MSB) ⊕ C_out(MSB). Example: 100 + 45 in 8 bits gives 10010001 = −111, overflow."""),
           ("Hamming code: the number of check bits",
            """To correct any single-bit error in a codeword of m data bits plus r check bits, the received word can be wrong in any of the m + r positions or not at all: m + r + 1 situations. The r check bits (syndrome) must distinguish all of them, and r bits give 2ʳ values, so 2ʳ ≥ m + r + 1. For m = 11 the smallest r is 4 (16 ≥ 16); for m = 4, r = 3 (8 ≥ 8). Check bits sit at positions that are powers of two; the syndrome, read as a binary number, is the position of the erroneous bit. A code with minimum distance d detects d − 1 errors and corrects ⌊(d − 1)/2⌋; adding an overall parity bit gives single-error correction and double-error detection (SECDED)."""),
       ],
       notes=[
           ("Conversions",
            """Integer part: divide repeatedly by the base and read the remainders from the last to the first. Fraction: multiply repeatedly by the base and read the integer parts in order; some finite decimal fractions (0.1) are infinite in binary. Between binary, octal and hexadecimal group the bits in threes and fours from the radix point. (1011.101)₂ = 8 + 2 + 1 + 0.5 + 0.125 = 11.625. The number of bits needed for N values is ⌈log₂ N⌉; a decimal number with d digits needs ⌈d·log₂ 10⌉ ≈ 3.32d bits. In BCD each digit uses 4 bits; BCD addition corrects any digit sum above 9 by adding 6. Excess-3 and Gray code are further binary codes; Gray(n) = n ⊕ (n >> 1)."""),
           ("Arithmetic details",
            """Subtraction A − B = A + (~B) + 1 in two's complement. Sign extension replicates the sign bit. Multiplication of two n-bit numbers gives a 2n-bit product; Booth's algorithm handles signed multiplication by recoding runs of ones. Shifting left multiplies by 2, an arithmetic right shift divides by 2 rounding towards −∞. A carry out of the top bit in unsigned addition signals unsigned overflow; the zero, sign, carry and overflow flags of the status register let branches test the results."""),
       ])

deepen("dl-floating-point",
       proofs=[
           ("Decoding and encoding a single-precision number step by step",
            """A normal number has the value (−1)ˢ × (1 + F/2²³) × 2^(E − 127) where S is the sign bit, E the 8-bit exponent field (1 … 254) and F the 23-bit fraction. To **decode** 0x41C80000: binary 0100 0001 1100 1000 0…; S = 0, E = 10000011₂ = 131 so the true exponent is 131 − 127 = 4; F = 1001000…0 so the mantissa is 1 + 0.5 + 0.0625 = 1.5625; the value is 1.5625 × 16 = 25. To **encode** −5.75: 5.75 = 101.11₂ = 1.0111 × 2², so S = 1, E = 2 + 127 = 129 = 10000001₂ and F = 0111 followed by zeros: 1 10000001 011100…0 = 0xC0B80000. The hidden leading 1 is why there are only 23 stored fraction bits for 24 bits of precision."""),
           ("Range, precision and the spacing of floating-point numbers",
            """The largest finite single-precision value has E = 254 and F = all ones: (2 − 2⁻²³) × 2¹²⁷ ≈ 3.4 × 10³⁸; the smallest positive normal is 2⁻¹²⁶ ≈ 1.18 × 10⁻³⁸ and the smallest denormal 2⁻¹⁴⁹. Between 2ᵉ and 2ᵉ⁺¹ there are 2²³ equally spaced numbers, so the spacing is 2ᵉ⁻²³: the absolute spacing doubles each time the exponent grows by one, but the **relative** spacing stays between 2⁻²⁴ and 2⁻²³. Hence integers up to 2²⁴ = 16,777,216 are exactly representable, and 16,777,217 is not (it rounds to 16,777,216). Machine epsilon (the gap above 1.0) is 2⁻²³ in single and 2⁻⁵² in double precision."""),
           ("Floating-point addition is not associative",
            """Add numbers by aligning exponents (shifting the smaller mantissa right), adding, normalising and rounding. Rounding can discard bits of the smaller operand. In single precision let a = 2²⁴ and b = c = 1: (a + b) + c: a + b = 2²⁴ + 1 rounds to 2²⁴ (the spacing is 2 there), and adding c again gives 2²⁴; but a + (b + c) = 2²⁴ + 2 = 16,777,218, which is representable. So (a + b) + c ≠ a + (b + c). Consequently compilers may not reorder floating-point operations freely, and sums should be accumulated from the small numbers to the large ones to reduce the rounding error."""),
       ],
       notes=[
           ("Special values",
            """Exponent field 0 with fraction 0: ±0; exponent 0 with non-zero fraction: **denormal** numbers, value (−1)ˢ × 0.F × 2^(−126) (no hidden bit, gradual underflow); exponent all ones with fraction 0: ±∞; all ones with non-zero fraction: NaN (not a number; NaN ≠ NaN). Double precision uses 11 exponent bits (bias 1023) and 52 fraction bits. Rounding modes: to nearest even (default), toward zero, toward +∞, toward −∞. Operations: 0/0 and ∞ − ∞ give NaN; x/0 gives ±∞ for non-zero x; comparing floating-point values with == is unsafe, use a tolerance."""),
           ("Typical GATE questions",
            """Convert a decimal fraction to IEEE 754 hexadecimal; decode a hex pattern; find the number of values between two numbers; determine the largest or smallest number for a given format (including non-IEEE formats with k exponent bits and bias 2ᵏ⁻¹ − 1, where the largest exponent field gives the largest number if no special values are reserved); compare ordering — IEEE positive floats compare in the same order as their bit patterns interpreted as unsigned integers, which is why the biased exponent is used."""),
       ])

deepen("dl-sequential-circuits",
       proofs=[
           ("The characteristic equations of the flip-flops",
            """**D:** Q⁺ = D. **T:** Q⁺ = T ⊕ Q (T = 1 toggles). **SR:** Q⁺ = S + R′Q with S R = 0: set when S = 1, reset when R = 1, hold otherwise. **JK:** J = 1, K = 0 sets; J = 0, K = 1 resets; J = K = 0 holds; J = K = 1 toggles: Q⁺ = JQ′ + K′Q. Check with the table for Q = 0: Q⁺ = J·1 + K′·0 = J; for Q = 1: Q⁺ = J·0 + K′·1 = K′. Conversions follow by equating characteristic equations: a T flip-flop from a JK needs J = K = T; a D flip-flop from a JK needs J = D and K = D′. The JK flip-flop's J = K = 1 toggling is the reason that a level-triggered JK latch 'races around' during the clock-high interval and must be edge-triggered."""),
           ("A ring counter has n states, a Johnson counter 2n states",
            """In an n-bit ring counter the last output feeds the first input and a single 1 circulates: states 1000, 0100, 0010, 0001 repeat after n clocks. In a Johnson (twisted-ring) counter the inverted last output feeds the first input. Starting from 0000 the sequence is 0000, 1000, 1100, 1110, 1111, 0111, 0011, 0001 and back to 0000: n ones shifted in followed by n zeros, so 2n states. A binary counter with n flip-flops has 2ⁿ states; a mod-N counter needs ⌈log₂ N⌉ flip-flops, and the unused states (2ᵏ − N) must be handled by self-correcting logic."""),
           ("Maximum clock frequency of a synchronous circuit and of a ripple counter",
            """Between two edge-triggered registers the data launched by one clock edge must arrive at the next register at least t_setup before the next edge: T ≥ t_clk→Q + t_logic(max) + t_setup, so f_max = 1/T. Hold-time safety needs t_clk→Q(min) + t_logic(min) ≥ t_hold. In a ripple (asynchronous) counter each stage is clocked by the previous stage's output, so the count settles only after the delays of all n stages add up: T ≥ n·t_pd. Four flip-flops with 10 ns delay each limit the clock to 1/40 ns = 25 MHz, whereas a synchronous counter's period is independent of n except for the carry logic."""),
       ],
       notes=[
           ("State machine design procedure",
            """Draw the state diagram from the specification (Moore: outputs on states; Mealy: outputs on transitions), build the state table, assign binary codes to the states, choose flip-flops and derive the excitation equations from the excitation tables (JK: 0→0 J = 0 K = X; 0→1 J = 1 K = X; 1→0 J = X K = 1; 1→1 J = X K = 0), derive the output equations and simplify with K-maps using the unused states as don't-cares. A sequence detector for 101 (overlapping) needs three Mealy states (no progress, seen 1, seen 10). The number of flip-flops is ⌈log₂ (number of states)⌉. Synchronous inputs are sampled at the clock edge; asynchronous inputs need synchronisers to avoid metastability."""),
           ("Registers and memory elements",
            """A latch is level-sensitive, a flip-flop edge-triggered (master–slave). A shift register of n flip-flops delays serial data by n clocks; a parallel-load register stores n bits. A register file combines registers with decoders and multiplexers; SRAM cells use six transistors and DRAM cells one transistor and a capacitor that needs refreshing. A frequency divider chain of T flip-flops divides the input clock by 2ⁿ at the nth output. A decade counter counts 0–9 and resets by decoding 1010."""),
       ])
